1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,359
PR review, cargo finance module. Author
injects with @Autowired DataSource
dataSource and marks the OLTP pool
@Primary. Reviewer insists on
@Resource(name = "reportingDataSource")
for the nightly export job.

2
00:00:15,479 --> 00:00:26,640
A third engineer pastes @Inject from a
Jakarta tutorial and claims it is "more
standard." The app has two beans of type
DataSource: oltpDataSource and
reportingDataSource.

3
00:00:26,759 --> 00:00:39,815
Which annotation wins is not taste — it is
injection algorithm. Pick the wrong
algorithm and the export job hammers the
OLTP pool during a full-table manifest
dump. Short rules of engagement.

4
00:00:39,935 --> 00:00:55,319
@Autowired (Spring) and @Inject
(Jakarta/JSR-330) are type-driven first.
If multiple candidates exist, Spring
narrows with @Qualifier, @Primary, or — in
some configurations — parameter/field
names.

5
00:00:55,439 --> 00:01:04,055
@Resource (Jakarta/JSR-250) is name-driven
first: default name is the field or setter
name, then it falls back to type.

6
00:01:04,175 --> 00:01:14,015
For two DataSources, name-based selection
is often the honest intent for "I want the
reporting pool," while @Primary expresses
"when someone asks for a DataSource
without narrowing,

7
00:01:14,135 --> 00:01:19,103
give them OLTP." Look at this java for a
moment.

8
00:01:19,223 --> 00:01:33,599
Walk the config. oltpDataSource method
name becomes the default bean name;
@Primary marks it as the preferred
candidate for ambiguous type matches.
reportingDataSource is a second bean of
the same type without primary.

9
00:01:33,719 --> 00:01:43,943
On ManifestExportJob, @Resource(name =
"reportingDataSource") skips the primary
game: look up that exact name in the
factory, inject that instance into the
field.

10
00:01:44,063 --> 00:01:55,967
The run method opens a connection from
whatever was injected — if you got
reporting, heavy reads stay off OLTP; if
you got OLTP by mistake, you will see lock
contention and slow checkouts while
exports run.

11
00:01:56,087 --> 00:02:05,879
Modern Spring style for the same intent
uses constructor injection plus @Qualifier
— type match, then name, without field
injection: Look at this java for a moment.

12
00:02:05,999 --> 00:02:17,015
Here @Autowired is often implicit on a
single constructor. Spring finds all
DataSource beans, filters to those
matching qualifier reportingDataSource,
and injects that one.

13
00:02:17,135 --> 00:02:31,343
The field name reporting no longer has to
match the bean name — the qualifier
carries intent. That is clearer in reviews
than relying on field-name defaults.
Runtime resolution, step by step, for
three common mistakes.

14
00:02:31,463 --> 00:02:43,007
Case A: @Autowired DataSource dataSource
on the export job, no qualifier. Spring
collects both beans by type, sees @Primary
on OLTP, injects OLTP.

15
00:02:43,127 --> 00:02:54,911
Symptom: export traffic on the primary DB,
pg_stat_activity shows long SELECT from
the export user on oltp host, checkout
latency climbs — no Spring error at
startup.

16
00:02:55,031 --> 00:03:05,135
Case B: @Resource on a field named
reportingDataSource without an explicit
name attribute — still resolves by field
name first, gets reporting even when OLTP
is primary.

17
00:03:05,255 --> 00:03:13,607
Case C: @Inject with
@Named("reportingDataSource") — behaves
like type-plus-qualifier, similar to
@Autowired + @Qualifier.

18
00:03:13,727 --> 00:03:25,751
Case D: @Autowired with field name
reportingDataSource but no @Qualifier and
multiple candidates — depending on Spring
version and spring.main-era defaults, you
may still get primary or an ambiguity
failure;

19
00:03:25,871 --> 00:03:29,855
do not bet production on name fallback for
@Autowired.

20
00:03:29,975 --> 00:03:44,519
Failure mode to drill in demos: ship Case
A to staging. Startup is green. First
nightly export coincides with peak
booking. On-call sees OLTP CPU spike, not
"wrong bean" in logs.

21
00:03:44,639 --> 00:03:58,367
Fix is @Qualifier or @Resource(name=...),
plus maybe removing accidental @Primary
misuse if primary was only added to
silence NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException
without thinking about export jobs. Trade-
offs.

22
00:03:58,487 --> 00:04:07,295
@Autowired/@Inject + @Qualifier keep
constructor injection and Spring idioms;
you must remember to qualify when
duplicates exist.

23
00:04:07,415 --> 00:04:13,679
@Primary reduces noise for the common
DataSource but is dangerous when secondary
uses are silent.

24
00:04:13,799 --> 00:04:23,615
@Resource makes name-first intent obvious
for fields/setters but pushes teams toward
field injection and JSR-250 semantics that
newcomers confuse with @Autowired.

25
00:04:23,735 --> 00:04:35,447
Prefer one house style: constructors +
@Qualifier for multiple beans of one type;
reserve @Primary for a true default; use
@Resource when aligning with Jakarta name-
based injection in mixed stacks.

26
00:04:35,567 --> 00:04:41,231
Misconception unique to these annotations:
"@Inject and @Autowired are identical,

27
00:04:41,351 --> 00:04:52,607
and @Resource is just the Jakarta spelling
of @Autowired." @Inject is close to
@Autowired but lacks some Spring-specific
features (for example required flag
semantics differ).

28
00:04:52,727 --> 00:05:02,711
@Resource is not a synonym — name-first
matching changes which bean you get when
duplicates exist. Finance exports finally
hit the reporting replica.

29
00:05:02,831 --> 00:05:12,023
A cinema ticketing team wants the opposite
extreme: no component scan at all, only
explicit @Configuration classes wiring the
full-screen box office.

30
00:05:12,143 --> 00:05:20,399
How those configuration classes themselves
are processed — enhanced, intercepted,
singleton-guaranteeing — is the next
mechanism to open.
